<h2>5. Database Design</h2>
<p>The SQLite database (<code>hostelhub.db</code>) contains three related tables. An allotment record links one resident to one specific room.</p>

<h3>5.1 Table: residents</h3>
<table>
  <tr><th>Column</th><th>Type</th><th>Constraints</th></tr>
  <tr><td>id</td><td>INTEGER</td><td>PRIMARY KEY, AUTOINCREMENT</td></tr>
  <tr><td>name</td><td>TEXT</td><td>NOT NULL</td></tr>
  <tr><td>email</td><td>TEXT</td><td>NULLABLE</td></tr>
  <tr><td>phone</td><td>TEXT</td><td>NULLABLE</td></tr>
  <tr><td>created_at</td><td>DATETIME</td><td>DEFAULT CURRENT_TIMESTAMP</td></tr>
</table>

<h3>5.2 Table: rooms</h3>
<table>
  <tr><th>Column</th><th>Type</th><th>Constraints</th></tr>
  <tr><td>id</td><td>INTEGER</td><td>PRIMARY KEY, AUTOINCREMENT</td></tr>
  <tr><td>name</td><td>TEXT</td><td>NOT NULL</td></tr>
  <tr><td>block</td><td>TEXT</td><td>NOT NULL</td></tr>
  <tr><td>capacity</td><td>INTEGER</td><td>DEFAULT 1</td></tr>
  <tr><td>created_at</td><td>DATETIME</td><td>DEFAULT CURRENT_TIMESTAMP</td></tr>
</table>

<h3>5.3 Table: allotments</h3>
<table>
  <tr><th>Column</th><th>Type</th><th>Constraints</th></tr>
  <tr><td>id</td><td>INTEGER</td><td>PRIMARY KEY, AUTOINCREMENT</td></tr>
  <tr><td>resident_id</td><td>INTEGER</td><td>NOT NULL, FOREIGN KEY to residents(id) ON DELETE CASCADE</td></tr>
  <tr><td>room_id</td><td>INTEGER</td><td>NOT NULL, FOREIGN KEY to rooms(id) ON DELETE CASCADE</td></tr>
  <tr><td>allotment_date</td><td>TEXT</td><td>NOT NULL</td></tr>
  <tr><td>return_date</td><td>TEXT</td><td>DEFAULT NULL</td></tr>
  <tr><td>created_at</td><td>DATETIME</td><td>DEFAULT CURRENT_TIMESTAMP</td></tr>
</table>

<h3>5.4 Relationships</h3>
<p>
The <code>allotments</code> table serves as the relational junction between <code>residents</code> and <code>rooms</code>. Foreign key enforcement guarantees that an allotment can only reference an existing resident and room, and deleting a resident or room automatically cascades and cleans up associated allotments.
</p>
<p>
When an allotment is active, <code>return_date</code> is <code>NULL</code>. When a resident vacates the room, <code>return_date</code> is stamped with the ISO timestamp. The database auto-seeds 6 rooms across Block A, Block B, and Block C, 4 residents, and initial allotments on startup so the API is immediately ready for evaluation.
</p>

<h2>6. REST API Design</h2>
<table>
  <tr>
    <th>Method</th>
    <th>Endpoint</th>
    <th>Description</th>
    <th>Success</th>
    <th>Errors</th>
  </tr>
  <tr><td>GET</td><td>/rooms</td><td>Get all rooms (includes dynamic occupancy status)</td><td>200</td><td>-</td></tr>
  <tr><td>GET</td><td>/rooms/:id</td><td>Get one room by ID</td><td>200</td><td>404</td></tr>
  <tr><td>POST</td><td>/rooms</td><td>Add a new room (name, block, capacity)</td><td>201</td><td>400</td></tr>
  <tr><td>PUT</td><td>/rooms/:id</td><td>Update an existing room (name, block, capacity)</td><td>200</td><td>400, 404</td></tr>
  <tr><td>DELETE</td><td>/rooms/:id</td><td>Delete a room</td><td>200</td><td>404</td></tr>
  <tr><td>GET</td><td>/rooms?block=Block A</td><td>Search rooms by block query parameter</td><td>200</td><td>-</td></tr>
  <tr><td>GET</td><td>/rooms/free</td><td>Warden helper: view only unoccupied rooms</td><td>200</td><td>-</td></tr>
  <tr><td>GET</td><td>/residents</td><td>Get all residents</td><td>200</td><td>-</td></tr>
  <tr><td>GET</td><td>/residents/:id</td><td>Get one resident by ID</td><td>200</td><td>404</td></tr>
  <tr><td>POST</td><td>/residents</td><td>Register a new resident (name, email, phone)</td><td>201</td><td>400</td></tr>
  <tr><td>GET</td><td>/allotments</td><td><strong>Join Endpoint:</strong> Allotments with resident_name and room_name</td><td>200</td><td>-</td></tr>
  <tr><td>POST</td><td>/allotments</td><td>Allot a room to a resident</td><td>201</td><td>400, 404, 409</td></tr>
  <tr><td>POST</td><td>/allotments/:id/return</td><td><strong>Twist:</strong> Return an allotment (sets return_date)</td><td>200</td><td>404, 409</td></tr>
  <tr><td>GET</td><td>/residents/:id/history</td><td><strong>Twist:</strong> List returned allotments for a resident</td><td>200</td><td>404</td></tr>
</table>

<h2>7. Implementation</h2>
<h3>7.1 Server and Routes (server.js — Part 1)</h3>
<p>
<code>server.js</code> initializes the Express application, activates JSON request body parsing, and mounts routes for <code>/rooms</code>, <code>/residents</code>, and <code>/allotments</code>. It also includes a discovery endpoint at <code>/</code> displaying project metadata and a catch-all 404 handler for undefined routes.
</p>

<h3>7.2 Database (server.js — Part 2)</h3>
<p>
This section establishes the connection to <code>hostelhub.db</code> (compatible with both <code>sqlite3</code> and Node.js built-in <code>node:sqlite</code>). It creates the <code>residents</code>, <code>rooms</code>, and <code>allotments</code> tables with foreign keys and automatically seeds sample records when the database is empty.
</p>

<h3>7.3 REST Endpoints (server.js — Part 3)</h3>
<p>
All 5 CRUD operations for rooms are implemented using parameterized queries (<code>?</code>) to prevent SQL injection. The mandatory multi-table SQL <code>JOIN</code> endpoint at <code>GET /allotments</code> combines allotments, residents, and rooms into a single readable payload.
</p>

<h3>7.4 Validation (server.js — Part 4)</h3>
<p>
Input validation middleware (<code>validateRoom</code>, <code>validateResident</code>, <code>validateAllotment</code>) verifies incoming request payloads before database queries execute. If <code>name</code> or <code>block</code> is missing or empty, it halts and returns HTTP <code>400 Bad Request</code> with a descriptive error message.
</p>

<h3>7.5 Search by Query Parameter</h3>
<p>
<code>GET /rooms?block=Block A</code> inspects <code>req.query.block</code> and appends <code>AND LOWER(r.block) LIKE ?</code> to the SQL query. <code>GET /rooms/free</code> filters out currently allotted rooms, directly solving the warden's paper-file problem.
</p>

<h3>7.6 Postman Collection and README</h3>
<p>
The collection (<code>HostelHub.postman_collection.json</code>) contains automated <code>pm.test()</code> assertions verifying HTTP status codes (200, 201, 400, 404, 409), JSON response structure, block search filters, and the Return Flow twist logic.
</p>

<h2>8. Twist: Return Flow</h2>
<p>
<strong>Requirement:</strong> <code>POST /allotments/:id/return</code> sets a return date. Returning twice gets <strong>409</strong> and the message <code>"Already returned"</code>. <code>GET /residents/:id/history</code> lists that resident's returned allotments.
</p>
<p>
<strong>Approach:</strong> Inside <code>POST /allotments/:id/return</code>, the allotment record is fetched. If <code>return_date</code> is already populated, the route halts and responds with HTTP <code>409 Conflict</code> and <code>{ "message": "Already returned" }</code>. If not returned, <code>return_date</code> is updated to the current timestamp and returns <code>200 OK</code>. <code>GET /residents/:id/history</code> queries all allotments for that resident where <code>return_date IS NOT NULL</code>, joined with room details.
</p>

<table>
  <tr>
    <th style="width: 15%;">Test</th>
    <th style="width: 30%;">Request</th>
    <th style="width: 25%;">Expected</th>
    <th style="width: 30%;">Actual Result</th>
  </tr>
  <tr>
    <td><strong>Test 1</strong></td>
    <td>POST /allotments/1/return</td>
    <td>200, return_date set</td>
    <td><strong>200 OK</strong>, allotment.return_date populated</td>
  </tr>
  <tr>
    <td><strong>Test 2</strong></td>
    <td>POST /allotments/1/return</td>
    <td>409, "Already returned"</td>
    <td><strong>409 Conflict</strong>, {"message":"Already returned"}</td>
  </tr>
  <tr>
    <td><strong>Test 3</strong></td>
    <td>GET /residents/1/history</td>
    <td>200, allotment 1 listed</td>
    <td><strong>200 OK</strong>, array containing returned allotment 1</td>
  </tr>
</table>

